  <!-- Creature Variants (#171)

       Regional name and colour variants of thirteen common creatures. Cosmetic only: a variant
       may differ in name, colour and flavour text, and in nothing else. No <stat>, no parts
       beyond Render and Description. Two identically-sized glyphs that behave differently with
       only colour to warn you reads as a bug when it kills you.

       Three mechanics carry this, and each is here for a measured reason.

       1. Tags INHERIT. A variant silently joins every DynamicObjectsTable its parent belongs to,
          and the paired :Weight tag binds only the one table it names. Left alone, the marsh dog
          spawns at full weight in the desert canyon, the hills, the mountains and the flowerfields
          - every biome except the one it is named after. So each variant keeps the single table
          its name argues for and carries Value="*delete" on the rest. That is the 72 delete tags
          below, and they are the whole reason the theming means anything.

       2. There is NO :Weight tag anywhere in this section, deliberately. Creature tables are never
          requested in the :Tier form, so they are built by FabricateDynamicObjectsTable, where the
          tier-delta bonus is unreachable and the base weight is exactly 1. :Weight is applied as a
          multiplier wrapped in (uint)Math.Ceiling, so ceil(1 x 0.25) = 1 and every fraction below
          one is inert. Vanilla's own Astral Tabby at 0.2 and Ixlthyxl at 0.1 do nothing either.
          A fractional weight here would read as tuning while changing nothing, which is worse than
          no weight at all. See docs/LESSONS.md.

       3. AggregateWith is what actually controls how often these appear. Members sharing a value
          are bundled into one pickone group whose outer weight is the MAX of its members, so a
          family occupies a single slot instead of one per member. Vanilla uses this on the snapjaws
          for exactly this purpose. Each variant aggregates with its vanilla parent, so the family
          stays precisely as common as it is in vanilla and these tables do not grow at all.
          Twenty-nine of the thirty-two add nothing anywhere; the marsh dog, rust beetle and salt
          beetle each keep a table their parent is not in, so those three add one slot apiece.

       The option (charter rule 6) is pure XML too. ExcludeFromDynamicEncountersOption is read by
       GameObjectBlueprint.IsExcludedFromDynamicEncounters, which resolves the value as an option
       ID and inverts it when the value is prefixed with "!". So "!OptionQudExpandedCECreatureVariants"
       means "excluded unless that option is Yes", and no C# is involved.

       Two caveats, both stated in the option's helptext. The blueprint caches the answer in a
       nullable bool that nothing ever resets, and a fabricated table is cached in
       PopulationManager.Populations once built - so the toggle governs tables built after it is
       set, not the zones already generated. And nothing in vanilla uses this tag, so it is an
       extension point the game supports but does not exercise; it needs confirming in a running
       game before release rather than on the strength of the decompile alone. -->

